There is a hidden gravity in the act of safeguarding. We speak of feathers as weightless, yet here they are an accumulation of care\'97a cumulative burden that slowly bows the spine.\
The protector\'92s body bears the toll of this devotion: a back hunched under the strain of being a shield, and fingers stained a raw, bruised red from plucking sweet melodies to quiet the storms of others. It is the quiet exhaustion of the guardian, whose bones know the anchor while the world sees only the wing. This is the ache of the guardian: the exhaustion of holding up a sky\
that everyone else assumes is weightless.}